WEBVTT

00:00.880 --> 00:04.000
Now experiments that took weeks take days.

00:04.800 --> 00:07.920
That can enable scientific breakthroughs much quicker.

00:09.680 --> 00:14.640
For the past year, we've been putting AI to work inside labs and manufacturing facilities.

00:16.160 --> 00:17.440
We kept hitting the same wall.

00:19.040 --> 00:22.560
There's no common way to connect a model to physical equipment.

00:22.560 --> 00:25.040
The model hardware standard changes that.

00:25.040 --> 00:30.880
MHS gives any AI model one standard way to connect with and operate devices.

00:30.880 --> 00:35.920
If you wanted your instruments to work together, say a camera and a stage of a microscope,

00:35.920 --> 00:38.720
you had to build a custom software integration between them.

00:39.440 --> 00:42.080
Every device you add complicates the picture.

00:42.720 --> 00:45.600
For complicated experiments, that's weeks of work.

00:46.480 --> 00:50.080
With MHS, each device connects once through one interface.

00:50.640 --> 00:52.560
The setup is accelerated by the agent.

00:52.560 --> 01:04.960
The agent has access to this context and can control operations.

01:06.000 --> 01:09.680
Here we're seeing Claude controlling a Leica microscope on its own.

01:09.680 --> 01:14.880
It's focusing the microscope, looking for a bacteria and deciding what to capture next.

01:15.920 --> 01:19.520
At Genentech, a scientist designed an experiment in a PDF,

01:19.520 --> 01:21.760
and dropped that PDF into Claude.

01:21.760 --> 01:24.560
Then Claude autonomously executed that experiment.

01:24.560 --> 01:28.080
When things went wrong, it was able to recover even overnight.

01:28.080 --> 01:35.840
The iteration cycle is much faster and the focus for me as a scientist shifts to actual scientific questions.

01:35.840 --> 01:41.120
Once everything is connected, running an experiment looks like you ask Claude to start it.

01:41.120 --> 01:45.680
Here we are looking at images of neurons in the brain.

01:45.680 --> 01:53.120
With MHS, I can instruct the microscope to move around, go deeper, or even take side views.

01:53.120 --> 01:53.920
All in real time.

01:53.920 --> 02:00.320
If you can test hypotheses faster, you could create general technologies faster,

02:00.880 --> 02:04.560
like finding new materials, also solving important scientific problems.

02:04.560 --> 02:07.440
This is how a century of progress can compress into a decade.

02:07.440 --> 02:12.800
The first time I've been able to do is to make a new model for a century of science.

